<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="what-is-aether-weaver" title="What Aether Weaver is">

    <show-structure for="chapter" depth="2"/>

    <p>
        Aether Weaver changes what a compiled class does, without touching its source.
    </p>

    <p>
        You write a weave: a plain Java class that says which classes it modifies and how. One
        engine reads it and rewrites their bytecode. The classes you are changing never learn
        about it, and neither does the code that calls them.
    </p>

    <code-block lang="mermaid">
flowchart TB
    W["The weave you write"] --> P["A plan, built once"]
    P --> E["The engine"]
    C["A class, handed over by a driver"] --> E
    E --> R["Rewritten bytecode"]
    </code-block>

    <chapter title="A weave is a class you write" id="a-weave">
        <p>
            Mark it <code>@Weave</code> and name the targets. Class literals if they are on the
            compile classpath, binary names as text if they are not. One of the two, never both.
        </p>

        <p>
            Inside it you put handlers and members:
        </p>

        <list>
            <li><p><code>@Inject</code> runs your code inside a target method,
                <code>@Redirect</code> replaces a call it makes, and <code>@Wrap</code> takes the
                call over.</p></li>
            <li><p><code>@Shadow</code> reaches a field or method the target already has.
                <code>@Unique</code> adds one that will not collide with it.</p></li>
        </list>

        <p>
            An instance weave is dissolved into every target it names, and the weave class
            itself never loads. A static weave stays where it is, and its handlers are called
            across the class boundary. <a href="annotations.topic">The annotation reference</a> has the
            rules that turn on that difference.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Four ways to run it" id="drivers">
        <p>
            The engine is the same in all four. What changes is who hands it the classes, and when.
        </p>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="The Maven plugin">
                <p>Weaves the classes your build has compiled. Nothing is needed at run time.</p>
            </def>
            <def title="The Java agent">
                <p>Weaves as classes load, under <code>-javaagent</code>. No build step.</p>
            </def>
            <def title="The weaving class loader">
                <p>Weaves inside a running application, for what it loads itself.</p>
            </def>
            <def title="The testkit">
                <p>Weaves in memory in a JUnit test, without redefining the class.</p>
            </def>
        </deflist>

        <p>
            The first three produce byte-identical output. A test weaves one fixture all three
            ways and compares the bytes. That is what says none of them weaves differently on its
            own. <a href="choose-a-driver.topic">Choose a driver</a> covers which one fits a project.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="What it is built on" id="built-on">
        <p>
            Bytecode goes through <code>java.lang.classfile</code>, the JDK's own Class-File API,
            and through nothing else. ASM, Javassist, Byte Buddy and cglib are banned dependencies,
            and the build fails if one appears.
        </p>

        <p>
            That API is the reason for the floor. Aether Weaver needs JDK 25 or later, and Maven
            3.9 or later to build against. <a href="install.topic">Install</a> lists the artefacts.
        </p>
    </chapter>

